izing that the onus is  really placed now on early diagnosed  early diagnosis of the disease so a  critical pain point and research in  medicine is really the discovery and the  validation of stable reproducible  biomarkers that come from minimally  invasive samples like plasma serum  saliva or urine proteins are the  traditional biomarkers and specifically  circulating proteins and plasma or serum  have been used or attempted to be used  for clinical screening of cancer some  examples of this are ca-125 that have  been used for ovarian cancer in women  and prostate serum antigen which has  used been used for prostate cancer in  men and of course these are single  molecule biomarkers and so theoretically  they are the most easily interpreted now  the problem with this is that as the  field progresses we are learning that  single protein biomarkers are really  proving to be insufficient to describe  very complex diseases like cancer and as  we learn more about disease we're  learning more about how the different  analyte classes interplay to not only  tell you what the current disease state  is in a patient but where the disease is  going so what we're learning now is that  biomarker signatures that utilize  multiple targets and more importantly  look across multiple analyte classes  will likely yield the most robust  diagnostic indicators for future  diagnosis so micro rna's are for the  last three years have become the  emerging biomarkers and in comparison to  their protein counterparts their ideals  and their ideal in many ways  specifically they have short known  sequences and they're largely conserved  across species they actually regulate  most of the genes in our bodies and  what's interesting about micro RNAs  is that a single micro RNA instead of  regulating a single gene can actually  regulate tens or even hundreds of genes  they're highly stable in biofluids  they're also highly stable for storage  reasons  so typically micro rna's are coupled  with prote