mensional volume this gives us  optimal thermodynamics but also gives us  a very large dynamic range for detection  and because the hydrogel is bio inert  this also allows us to detect directly  from crude biofluids without the need to  do any blocking step in BSA or anything  else again we have a very versatile  chemistry that we can apply to the  particle and this really allows us to  adapt the particles the specific need  and to use them across different target  classes and again as we already  mentioned we perform readout on a  standard flow cytometry and the way this  is done is that our rod shape will flow  past the detection zone of a site ometer  which would then think that the rod is  actually comprised of three closely  spaced cells that are going by the  interrogation region so each one of our  particles that goes through a flow  cytometry is detected as three events  that is later decoded using our software  so to have a quick look at what the  software offering is we provide instant  bioinformatics with our fire flex  analysis workbench now this is a Java  based piece of software that really runs  anywhere it has simple point-and-click  GUI and it doesn't require any any  scripts this will allow you to layout  your experiment be it across multiple  targets and multiple plates that allow  you to easily import those data for a  quick interpretation and analysis we  allow a broad range of visualization  from heat maps to bar plots to volcano  plots and also provide statistical  information as far as ANOVA analysis  across cohorts we talk about p-values  and we also provide corrected p-values  that take into account the multiplexing  that is performed in these assays all of  the images are a publication ready and  they can be scaled to meet your needs  another really interesting piece of  software that we're developing and this  is really for researchers who are unsure  of which targets they want to they want  to quantify in their multiplex assay we  have the fire flex discovery engine and  what this allows researchers to do is to  type in their field of interest and with  the click of a button what the software  does it will actually analyze the most  recent publications in that area to  provide a word cloud of the micro rna's  that have been most recently published  that will provide the the genes of  interest that are also published in  those papers and they'll also provide  you a list of authors that are actively  publishing in the area and all of these  microRNAs and keywords are clickable  such that you can dig in deeper and see  how those micro rna's were referenced in  the various papers or to see an  annotated list of the publications just  I encourage you to check that out now  we'll talk a little bit more about the  nuts and bolts of we have a platform but  how do we actually use the platform to  quantify micro RNAs and proteins  this is a snapshot of the protocol that  we use for micro RNA detection and it's  really broken into four main stages and  the first stage we will hybridize micro  RNAs and a crude digest of a sample with  our micro particles in the well of a  microtiter plate we will then perform a  labeling step we will then allude off  the labeled micro RNAs and amplify it  via universal PCR will then transfer the  amplicon back on to our particles where  we will hybridize add a reporter for  fluorescence and scan them on again a  standard flow cytometer and lastly as I  mentioned these flow cytometer files can  be loaded into our software for  instantaneous analysis now we'll take a  look at the molecular workflow in the  probe region of our micro RNA detection  particles we have an all of a nucleotide  probe that has three regions on it the  central region of the probe is specific  for a given micro RNA target and the to  end regions are specific for universal  adapters that will then be ligated on 2d  micro rna's so in the first step again  we are capturing a specific micro RNA  onto each spe