s quite a  bit about lung cancer but just to point  out a few things  lung cancer accounts for about a third  of all cancer related deaths and with a  five-year survival rate of only 2% it's  a pretty difficult disease to deal with  now chemotherapy is known to improve  clinical outcome of non-small-cell lung  cancer but as is the case with many  cancers it's really dependent on the  correct disease classification and  patient stratification  there have been some previous studies  that have indicated that micro RNA  signatures could potentially be used to  classify and prognosis biomarkers by the  prognostic biomarkers of non-small cell  lung cancer and so the aim of this study  was to really dig deeper into that and  to perform a multiply multi-site  prospective clinical trial with the main  goal of really identifying circulating  microRNAs that can be used as prognostic  markers of overall survival now  alongside this there were a couple other  goals of the study and they were to  identify micro RNA signatures that were  potentially related to disease  stabilisation they looked at micro RNAs  that could be indicative of tumor  shrinkage or the time to disease  progression following chemotherapy so  just very briefly these studies were  performed using patch teen blood again  they looked at 50 patients and they  monitor those patients over time and of  the key findings in this study what they  did is a traditional biomarker  development where they looked at  thirteen hundred and forty seven micro  rna's in the discovery phase and from  those they identified ten micro rna's  that were associated with overall  survival and most significantly of those  was micro na 29a and as shown in the  kaplan-meier plot to the right those  patients that had a very high expression  of mer 29a and circulation actually had  the the poorest outcome secondly they  found 12 12 micro rna's that were  associated with tumor shrinkage and of  those the most significant was micro na  665 as 